practice.solution:
export type Order = { id: string; createdAt: Date; totalCents: number };

export type ApiField<T> = T extends Date ? string : T;

export type Jsonified<T> = { [K in keyof T]: ApiField<T[K]> };

export function toJson(order: Order): Jsonified<Order> {
  return { id: order.id, createdAt: order.createdAt.toISOString(), totalCents: order.totalCents };
}

const order: Order = { id: "ord_1001", createdAt: new Date("2026-10-01T09:00:00Z"), totalCents: 4599 };
const json = toJson(order);

console.log(`Placed at ${json.createdAt}`);
console.log(JSON.stringify(json));

steps.body:
When the type a condition checks is a bare type parameter, and the caller passes a union, the condition runs once for each member, and the results are joined back into a union. That's called distribution:

```typescript types
type ToArray<T> = T extends unknown ? T[] : never;

type Lists = ToArray<string | number>;
//   ^? type Lists = string[] | number[]
```

`string | number` is checked as `string`, giving `string[]`, then as `number`, giving `number[]`. Every type fits `unknown`, so this condition is always true. It's there only to make the type distribute.

Distribution is also how a condition filters a union: a member that becomes `never` drops out, because `never` vanishes from any union. That's how `Exclude` removes members.

To check a union as a whole instead, wrap both sides in square brackets: `[T] extends [unknown]`. A one-item tuple isn't a bare type parameter, so nothing distributes.

examples.code:
type ToArray<T> = T extends unknown ? T[] : never;

type Column = ToArray<string | number>;
//   ^? type Column = string[] | number[]

function describe(column: Column): string {
  return `${column.length} values: ${column.join(", ")}`;
}

const names: Column = ["Ada", "Grace", "Linus"];
const scores: Column = [91, 78];

console.log(describe(names));
console.log(describe(scores));

examples.explanation:
`ToArray` distributes, so a `Column` is a list of strings or a list of numbers, as the bubble shows. Each column holds one kind of value, and a mixed one doesn't compile:

```typescript error
type ToArray<T> = T extends unknown ? T[] : never;

const mixed: ToArray<string | number> = ["Ada", 91];
```

`tsc` reports TS2322, because `(string | number)[]` is neither of the two list types. `length` and `join` exist on both kinds of list, so `describe` can use them without narrowing first.

examples.code:
type MyExclude<T, U> = T extends U ? never : T;

type Status = "draft" | "review" | "published" | "archived";

type Live = MyExclude<Status, "draft" | "archived">;
//   ^? type Live = "published" | "review"
type BuiltIn = Exclude<Status, "draft" | "archived">;
//   ^? type BuiltIn = "published" | "review"

const BADGES: Record<Live, string> = {
  review: "In review",
  published: "Live",
};

const posts: { title: string; status: Live }[] = [
  { title: "Mapped types", status: "published" },
  { title: "Conditional types", status: "review" },
];

for (const post of posts) {
  console.log(`${post.title}: ${BADGES[post.status]}`);
}

type ToArray<T> = T extends unknown ? T[] : never;
type ToMixedArray<T> = [T] extends [unknown] ? T[] : never;

type Column = ToArray<string | number>;
//   ^? type Column = string[] | number[]
type Row = ToMixedArray<string | number>;
//   ^? type Row = (string | number)[]

const header: Row = ["name", "age", "city"];
const row: Row = ["Ada", 36, "London"];

console.log(header.join(","));
console.log(row.join(","));

