steps.body:
Inside the `extends` part of a condition, `infer U` declares a new type variable, and TypeScript fills it in from whatever is in that spot of the matched type. The true branch can then use it:

```typescript types
type Unwrap<T> = T extends Promise<infer U> ? U : T;

type A = Unwrap<Promise<string>>;
//   ^? type A = string
type B = Unwrap<number>;
//   ^? type B = number
```

`Promise<string>` matches `Promise<infer U>` with `U` as `string`. A `number` doesn't match, so it comes back unchanged. A type variable from `infer` exists only in the true branch.

The utility types that take functions apart are written this way. The library declares `ReturnType<T>` as `T extends (...args: any) => infer R ? R : any`: match a function type, and name its return type `R`. `Parameters<T>` puts `infer P` in place of the whole parameter list, which is why it gives a tuple.

examples.code:
type Unwrap<T> = T extends Promise<infer U> ? U : T;

async function loadUser(id: number) {
  return { id, name: id === 1 ? "Ada" : "Grace" };
}

type User = Unwrap<ReturnType<typeof loadUser>>;
//   ^? type User = { id: number; name: string; }

type Nested = Promise<Promise<boolean>>;
type OneLevel = Unwrap<Nested>;
//   ^? type OneLevel = Promise<boolean>
type AllLevels = Awaited<Nested>;
//   ^? type AllLevels = boolean

function label(user: User): string {
  return `#${user.id} ${user.name}`;
}

console.log(label(await loadUser(1)));
console.log(label(await loadUser(2)));

type ElementOf<T> = T extends readonly (infer E)[] ? E : never;

const SIZES = ["S", "M", "L", "XL"] as const;

type Size = ElementOf<typeof SIZES>;
//   ^? type Size = "L" | "M" | "S" | "XL"
type Score = ElementOf<number[]>;
//   ^? type Score = number
type NotAList = ElementOf<string>;
//   ^? type NotAList = never

const PRICES: Record<Size, number> = { S: 18, M: 20, L: 22, XL: 25 };

for (const size of SIZES) {
  console.log(`${size}: $${PRICES[size]}`);
}

type FirstArg<F> = F extends (first: infer A, ...rest: never[]) => unknown ? A : never;

function sendWelcome(user: { name: string; email: string }, template: string): string {
  return `${template} to ${user.name} <${user.email}>`;
}

type Recipient = FirstArg<typeof sendWelcome>;
//   ^? type Recipient = { name: string; email: string; }

const recipients: Recipient[] = [
  { name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com" },
  { name: "Grace", email: "grace@example.com" },
];

for (const r of recipients) {
  console.log(sendWelcome(r, "welcome-v2"));
}

